what's up everyone bang bang will  clemente and i didn't get a chance to  sit down this week as we usually do for  our weekly conversation so i'm gonna  break down a couple of the charts that  he included in his weekly blockwear  email first let's take a look there's  been a three month long downtrend dating  all the way back to november for  bitcoin's price it recently went ahead  and broke out of that now it doesn't  mean that we're in the clear there's  still a number of different resistances  that we need to claim the first being  right around forty thousand dollars  seven hundred but then we've gotta get  the forty seven thousand dollars both of  those important numbers in terms of  bitcoin continuing in a bull market  momentum then if we jump and we look at  the open interest what we can see is  that bitcoin's price has been drawing  down over the last three months but that  open interest has remained relatively  high normally when price draws down  you'd expect that there'll be a wipeout  of the open interest we haven't seen  that and so that could tell us one of  two things either there's still some  leverage in the system or what's more  likely is that there's some leverage but  it's on the short side that people could  be hedging their positions and therefore  it's worth paying attention to not  something to be fearful of yet but  definitely not what you would expect  when the price gets wiped out the way  that it has then if we jump all the way  and we start looking at actually the  open interest using bitcoin as  collateral what we can see is that  traders have been switching out their  bitcoin that used to be used as  collateral for stable coins now this is  important because ultimately if they're  using bitcoin as collateral it's got  this negative convexity to it there's a  very high probability that you could see  kind of these washouts these  liquidations where price goes down very  very significantly very very quickly but  now all of a sudden when you start to  actually migrate from bitcoin to stable  coins in those margin contracts is a  much healthier backdrop for the mark for  the market structure from a macro  perspective you would expect in these  situations to have less volatility to  the downside stable coins are stable  hence the name stable coins and so  bitcoin as collateral ends up being a  little bit more risky from a price  standpoint if we then jump and will  breaks down the on-chain metrics using  the entity-adjusted a-sol metric this is  a seven-day moving average and what he's  showing here is that the younger and  younger the coin is the more likely it  is being sold and so this is exactly  what we saw in 2021 basically from  february and march all the way through  the summer until july and august we saw  a big price drawdown and all it was was  the short-term holders those people who  had just bought bitcoin the weak hands  they were turning and they were selling  they were dumping their coins in the  market and they're being bought up by  the people who've been around the  veterans the strong hands and that's  what we continue to see here in this  metric then if we jump and we actually  look well what the hell are the  short-term holders doing these people  have held bitcoin for less than 155 days  what you can see is that the short-term  holder realized price continues to trend  down that means that those coins that  were previously bought at higher prices  are now being sold at a loss the  short-term holders have weak hands  they're selling their coins at a loss  and they're getting out of the market  then if we go and we look what the  short-term holders are selling what are  the whales doing as you would expect the  whales are gobbling up as much bitcoin  as possible they continue to accumulate  more and more and more and we can see  that those whales every time they start  to buy in the past we have seen a  massive price increase in the future  history may not necessarily repeat but  